# GC Cancer Block Week 1: General Principles of Radiation Oncology <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **Principles:**
  - Ionising radiation - EM radiation from beta- gamma photons and charged particles
  - Tumour control not achieved by direct cell kill, but by producing secondary electrons causing double-strand DNA beraks in nuclei leading to cell death
  - Radiation falls off following inverse-square law but never drops to zero
  - Gray - unit of RT
  - Duplicating chromosomes more vulnerable to radiation as during DNA replication, unwinding of DNA from dsDNA to ssDNA - hence more susceptible and accounts for differential response (eradicates tumour cells while minimising damage to surrounding normal tissue)
  - 
- **Goals of RT** - eradicates tumour cells (neoplastic vs non neoplastic \[most commonly GO\], benign \[vestibular schwannoma\] vs malignant tumours) while minimising damage to surrounding normal tissue
- **Therapeutic ratio** - consider a radiation dose-response curve:
  - Differential response to effect of radiation by tumour cells and normal cells (diff between effect on tumour and normal cells is termed <u>therapeutic ratio</u>)
  - We aim to maximise therapeutic ratio - graphical representation of the goal of RT:
    - Fully make use of the difference of cell killing and regeneration capacities of tumour cells and normal cells to achieve the maximum therapeutic ratio
  - Description of the differential dose-response curve between tumour and normal cells:
    - 0 point - no effect on tumour
- **Differential response to RT:**
- **How is RT provided:**
  - Linear accelerator - electron gun connected to high -ve voltage will produce fast shooting electrons? with magnet to accelerate electrons
  - Tungsten converts electron to x-ray and bent 90 degrees to release via Gantry –\> directed at patient on treatment couch
  - Tomotherapy (see section on IMRT)
  - Volumetric modulated RT therapy machine (contains cone-beam CT scanner)
- **Pretreatment workup:**
  - Dx, clinical evaluation and staging
  - Approach to Mx for tumour - surgical, chemo, RT, targeted/ immunotherapy, vs multimodal approach
  - Role of RT (Tx intent) - radical vs palliative
  - RT planning - if RT considered:
    - Patient immobilisation (prevent irradiation of important structures e.g. brainstem, optic nerve)
    - Image acquisition of tumour
    - Choice of technique and beam modification
    - Calculation of dose distribution
- **Immobilisation:**
  - Thermoplastic cast - when subjected to hot temperature, becomes soft, and placed and molded to the Tx site
- **Image acquisition of tumour** - draw what to irradiate and what structures to preserve:
  - X-ray traditionally, but limited
  - 2D radiation technique is no longer used due to unnecessary irradiation of vital structures, especially in context of H&N tumours
  - MRI and PET-CT co-registration in contouring and detection of subtle synchronous tumour (precision RT see below)
- Achieve better therapuetic ratio by advanced RT techniques (i.e. allows for even lower doses)
- New modalities:
  - Intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT)
  - Image-guided radiation therapy (IGRT)
  - Cyberknife
  - Stereotatic radiosurgery
  - Charged-particle therapy
  - Brachytherapy
- **IMRT:**
  - <u>Principles</u> - multiple beams directed to the tumour at different angles, with modulation of intensity of radiation dose of each bene
    - Multiple radiation beam coming from different angles but all aiming at the tumour - individually each beam provides a small radiation dose, but aggregate at the tumour such that the tumour is subjected to higher dose
    - Since each beam uses low radation dose - this minimises radiatio to normal structures
    - Creates dose gradient to the tumour and surrounding normal structures (i.e. high dose to tumour, low dose to normal tissue)
  - <u>Tumour delineation</u> - by contrast CT, MRI/ PET, rather than conventional radiograph, require **giving a margin** to account for 1) occult microscopic spread, 2) physiological motion, 3) machine setup error
  - <u>Device</u> - tomotherapy:
    - Helical RT delivery system that integrates linear accelerator and CT scan technology
- VMAT?
- Comarison between 2D RT and 3D RT: 
![](~/Documents/notes/img/comparison_between_2drt_and_3d_rt.png)
- **IGRT:**
  - <u>Principles</u> - real-time imaging before each Tx to detect and correct set-up error (esp. patient and tumour can still move 1-2mm in all directions despite casting):
    - Calculates overlap with pre-treatment imaging –\> good overlap indicates good positioning
    - Monitoring body contour - weight loss as a measure of S/E (i.e. RT-induced mucositis can result in loss of appetite and weight loss), enabling motion during immobisation (comprimises effects of RT)
  - <u>Devices</u>:
    - MV linear accelerator and detector
    - kv cone-beam CT and kv detector
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - tumours with undetectable organ motions during Tx or site with dramatic change in body and contour
    - Prostate CA (e.g. position varies depending on bladder and rectum)
    - H&N tumours (esp LN +ve tumours) as it shrinks rap0idly with RT or chemoirradiation, and also weight loss as S/E requiring imaging to assess overlap
- **Cyberknife** - linear accelerator w/ robotic arm and a small gantry:
  - Long RT delivery time because of small gantry size and thus small area of irradiation
  - Indications:
    - small tumours close to normal critical structures
    - Cord compression
    - Brain metastasis
    - tumours close to neurostructures (e.g. brainstem, optic nerve)
- **Sterotactic radiosurgery** (SRS) - improved IMRT but more dangerous?
  - Other names:
    - X-knife
    - Gamma-knife
  - <u>Principles</u> - multiple x-ray or gamma rays from different angles, delivering high doses in one fraction (18-20 Gy) or few fractions (SRT)
  - <u>Limitations</u>:
    - Very complication set up for immobilisation
    - Long RT delivery time
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - Brain metastasis (max 3, \<3cm each)
    - Meningioma
    - Acoustic neuroma
    - Carvenous haemangioma
    - Glomus jugulare
    - AVM
- **Charge-particle therapy:**
  - Makes use of other type of ionising radiation (charged particle), cf x-ray, gamma-ray in IMRT, tomotherapy, SRS etc.
  - Types:
    - Proton
    - Carbon
    - Neon
  - Device - cyclotron (extremely large device, half a football pitch), gantry is so huge takes up the whole room
  - Concept of Bragg Peak:
    - Does not follow inverse-square law depicting relationship between intensity and tissue depth
    - Characteristic Bragg peak, remains low radiation therapy outside a certain depth known as Bragg Peak 
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/bragg_peak_of_charged_particle_therapy.png)
  - Extra high dose of radiation to centre of tumour
  - Clinical indications:
    - Small tumours extremely close to vital structures where rapid dose fall-off is essential to avoid permanent damage
    - Brain stem
    - Orbit, optic nerve, chiasm, pituitary
    - Spinal cord
    - Brainstem
- **Brachytherapy:**
  - Short distance
  - Types:
    - Interstitial brachytherapy (H&N, breast recurrence after BCT)
    - Intracavitary brachytherapy (cervical, breast, persistent NPC)
    - Endoluminal brachytherapy (lung, rectal)

  
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/external_vs_brachy.png)
- **Selective internal radiation therapy:**
  - Radioactive endovascular therapy that uses microspheres to ionizing radiation to hepatic tumours (HCC or liver mets)
  - Principles of microspheres:
    - Extremely small (30-35 micrometers), size of diameter of feeding artery
    - Radiolabelled w/ yttrium 90 - high dose of beta-rays (2.3 MeV), short half life (64h) minimal penetration (11mm)
    - Good therapeutic ratio:
      - Therapeutic doses to tumour but tolerable by normal liver parenchyma
  - Used in combination w/ other local therapy:
    - Ablative therapy - percutaneous ethanol injection, RFA
    - TACE
  - S/E:
    - N/V - if microspheres lodged in gastroduodenal artery
    - Gastritis/ duodenitis
    - Radiation-induced liver injury
    - Radiation pancreatitis
    - Radiation-fibrosis of the lungs due to lung shunting (left lower lobe)
- Read up NET treated w/ radioligand therapy w/ lutetium 177 somatostatic analogue (see slides)
- **Combination w/ other theraies:**
  - w/ targeted therapy - radiation increases experssion of EGFR, and blockade of EGFR signalling sensitize to effects of radiation (Cetuximab increases therapeutic ratio of RT)
  - w/ immunotherapy (radioimmunotherapy) - Mab conjugated w/ radiolabelled substance (e.g. ibritumomab, Anti-CD20 conjugated to yttrium 90 \[for non-Hodgkin lymphoma\], tositumomab, Anti-CD20 conjugated to iodine-131)
  - w/ chemotherapy (chemoradiation) - chemotherapy acts as radiosensitisers to potentiate effect of RT:
    - a/w more acute and long-term but manageable S/E (e.g. mucositis and pain)
    - Only few chemo agents used concurrently:
      - Cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin
      - 5-FU, capecitabine (antimetabolites)
      - Paclitaxel
      - Temozolomide
      - Etoposide
      - Mitomycin
      - Vincristine
    - Never use anthracycline as they cause severe radiosensitizing S/E (e.g. skin necrosis)
- **Abscopal effect of radiotherapy** (see slides for case report) - effects of RT not limited to prescribed radiation field:
  - Increased presentation of Ag by myeloid cells within the tumour stroma and therapy enhancing T-cell killing of tumor cell (esp melanoma, RCC, lymphoma)
- **S/E** - acute vs long term, must localise to site of RT:
  - **Acute:**
  - **Chronic** (last for \> 6mo)
- Factors:
  - Total dose
  - Dose in each fraction
  - Number of fractions
  - Technique (modern less S/E than conventional 2D)
  - Other treatments given concurrently with radiotherapy
  - Susceptibility and sensitivity of normal organ to radiation S/E

# GC Cancer Block Week 1: Cancer Imaging <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **Role of imaging:**
  - Screening (e.g. mammogram)
  - Dx
  - Staging
  - For Tx - e.g. surgical planning, RT therapy
  - Surveillance:
    - Assessment of Tx response
    - For recurrence
- **Imaging modalities:**
  - Radiation hazard - radiograph, CT, PET/CT, nuclear medicine
  - Non-ionizing radiation - MRI, USG
